## How fast a program runs

25 of the benchmark set's 29 programs, built on cig 1.24.0 and timed on one machine beside `cig run` and the same programs written in Python (all 25), JavaScript (24), Lua (21) and Rust (8), every run's output checked. Every number here is made from the run's raw samples by a script and made again by a second one written apart from it ([the paper](docs/BENCHMARKS-2026-11-15.md)). A cell is the geometric mean, over the compute programs both have ({{programs.compute}} at most), of the program's median time divided by the other's, so under 1 means faster; in brackets, how many of those programs it was faster, level and slower on:

{{table:ratios-compute}}

- **A stage `b` program** (`cigcompile build --stage b`, the script's bytecode on cig's VM) takes {{geomean.compute.stage-b.cig}} of `cig run`'s time, faster on all {{n.compute.stage-b.cig}}, and {{geomean.compute.stage-b.python}} of CPython 3.13's; it starts in {{median.hello.stage-b}} ms, where CPython takes {{median.hello.python}} ms.
- **A stage `a` program**, the default, runs on the interpreter as `cig run` does ({{geomean.compute.stage-a.cig}} of its time).
- **Lua is faster**: a stage `b` program takes {{geomean.compute.stage-b.lua}} times Lua 5.4's time and {{geomean.compute.stage-b.luajit}} times LuaJIT's.

Whole processes, start-up included, on a 2018 laptop on battery.
